 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel requires a constant number of operations (lookup, interpolation, triangle generation). The lookup is $O(1)$.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel requires a constant number of operations (lookup, interpolation, triangle generation). The lookup is $O(1)$ [S2, S3, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for the lookup tables (fixed size: 256 entries). The output mesh size depends on the surface area.
-*   **Performance:** Highly efficient on GPUs due to branchless execution (if using the Triangle Table directly or bit-packed tables) and coalesced memory access [S3, unofficial]. The Edge Table variant requires an additional step to identify edges before interpolation, which may introduce branching if not optimized [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The compute shader dispatch and synchronization are described in [S3, unofficial]. The loop structure iterates through lookup table entries and checks for a terminator, so it is not strictly branchless in all implementations, but the table lookup itself avoids runtime topology decisions [S2, S3, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, fast, deterministic topology, well-understood.
 *   **Limitations:** Does not guarantee topological correctness in ambiguous cases (cracks). The resulting mesh may have more vertices/triangles than necessary compared to [Dual Contouring](778-dual-contouring.md). Precision issues near the isovalue can cause artifacts [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Marching Cubes algorithm was introduced by William Lorensen and Harvey Cline in 1987 [S2, S3, unofficial]. The lookup tables are a core part of their original implementation, precomputing the 256 cases to avoid runtime decision trees. This method has become the standard for isosurface extraction in medical imaging and scientific visualization. The GPU implementations (e.g., [S3, unofficial]) adapt this CPU-centric table lookup to parallel compute shaders, leveraging the fixed-size nature of the tables for efficient memory coalescing.
